{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   The user (via the cobbler orchestrator) is executing stitch task #5443: \"[stitch] cmd/expr: srd066 R4.1-R4.4\". The task requires implementing the exit codes and differential testing requirements (R4 group) for the `cmd/expr` utility. Specifically:\n   - R4.1: Exit 0 when expression evaluates to non-null, non-zero value\n   - R4.2: Exit 1 when expression evaluates to null or 0\n   - R4.3: Exit 2 on syntax error/missing operand/division by zero; exit 3 on internal error\n   - R4.4: Comprehensive differential tests covering all expr operations, comparing stdout, stderr emptiness, and exit codes against gexpr reference binary\n\n   The task specifies modifying `cmd/expr/main.go` and `cmd/expr/expr_test.go` only. R1-R3 groups are already implemented. Design decisions: extend existing code (don't rewrite), use exec.LookPath(\"gexpr\") + t.Skip pattern, set LC_ALL=C in tests.\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - Differential testing pattern via `pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests`\n   - GNU expr exit code semantics (0=true/nonzero, 1=false/null/zero, 2=syntax error, 3=internal error)\n   - BRE (Basic Regular Expression) to Go regex conversion\n   - Recursive descent parser for expr (parseOr → parseAnd → parseComparison → parseAddSub → parseMulDiv → parsePrimary)\n   - SIGPIPE handler via `sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()`\n   - `testutils.NormalizeFunc` for normalizing binary name differences in stderr between gexpr and expr\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - `docs/specs/software-requirements/srd066-expr.yaml`\n      - The SRD specification for expr. R4 group (lines 53-63) defines exit code requirements and differential testing mandate.\n      - R4.1: exit 0 for non-null/non-zero; R4.2: exit 1 for null/0; R4.3: ex